{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms without smuggling in induction, provided it is framed strictly as a methodological rule based on the asymmetry of risk rather than a prediction of future success.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly stated that acting on corroboration requires an inductive assumption about the uniformity of nature.","If the preference for corroborated theories can only be justified by assigning them a higher subjective probability of future success.","If the 'asymmetry of risk' argument implicitly relies on the premise that past performance indicates future reliability."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"193e9093bea201f6","quote":"The preference is defensible on Popperian terms through the asymmetry of risk: rejecting a well-corroborated"},{"block":"07785266ae5808f5","quote":"The conjecture predicts that a consistent Popperian agent can prefer Theory A over T"},{"block":"08c37be0a0e09d91","quote":"The conjecture forbids the interpretation that corroboration serves as a pro"}],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which corroboration does not alter regarding probability) and the pragmatic decision of which theory to test or act upon. The mechanism relies on the 'asymmetry of risk': rejecting a well-corroborated theory in favor of a less tested one exposes the agent to a higher risk of immediate falsification without gaining any corresponding increase in informational content or testability. Therefore, choosing the better-corroborated theory is a rational application of the critical method to minimize the chance of error in the present moment, not an inductive inference that the theory will hold true in the future.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}